{#
  News mechanism research pass (2026-08-24). A pure "freshest first"
  sort (buildNewsPool's prior shape) satisfies "newest on top" but
  throws away relevance entirely -- a routine, 1-importance headline
  from 2 minutes ago permanently outranks a major, 5-importance story
  from 40 minutes ago. A pure importance sort has the opposite problem
  (a big story from 3 days ago never leaves the top). Real news
  products don't pick one axis -- they combine relevance and recency
  into a single score that decays over time, the same shape Reddit's
  "hot" ranking and Hacker News' own points/(age)^gravity formula use.
  This project already has its own established convention for exactly
  this kind of decay -- sentiment.py's score_messages() and
  wallstreet.py's _score_rating_momentum() both use exponential
  half-life decay, not a power law -- reused here rather than
  introducing a second decay shape into the codebase.

  Tiers don't share one native importance scale: Material Events have
  none at all (a filing is material by regulatory definition, not
  graded 1-5), Company News has a real Gemini-assigned 1-5, Market
  News is deliberately unranked ambient context (see PRODUCT.md's News
  Intelligence System -- three tiers, each answering a different
  question). NEWS_BASELINE_IMPORTANCE anchors each tier onto one
  shared comparable scale without pretending a precise per-item score
  exists where none was ever computed.

  Shared here (not hand-copied) because dashboard_template.html's
  cross-tier feed and stock_template.html's per-ticker Company News
  list both need the identical ranking, and this project has already
  been burned once by the same logic drifting between hand-copied
  <script> blocks (see SITE_REDESIGN_RESET.md's Phase 0 finding).
#}
const NEWS_RELEVANCE_HALF_LIFE_HOURS = 6; // roughly a trading day: a big story should still lead for a few hours, but day-old news shouldn't outrank anything fresh by the next session
const NEWS_BASELINE_IMPORTANCE = { material: 4, company: 3, market: 2.5 };
const NEWS_BREAKING_MINUTES = 90;

// Company News (yfinance/Finnhub) publishes ISO 8601 ("2026-08-22T12:33:00Z");
// Market News (RSS feeds, Finnhub general news) publishes RFC 822
// ("Sat, 22 Aug 2026 13:01:00 GMT") or ISO -- a plain string sort mixes
// formats and produces garbage ordering (an RFC-822 string starting with
// a weekday name doesn't compare correctly against an ISO string).
// Date.parse() handles both correctly; anything unparseable sorts as
// "no known time" (null age) rather than crashing or landing at a
// misleading position.
function newsTs(iso) {
  const t = Date.parse(iso || '');
  return isNaN(t) ? 0 : t;
}

function newsAgeHours(iso, now) {
  const ts = Date.parse(iso || '');
  if (isNaN(ts)) return null;
  return Math.max(0, ((now || Date.now()) - ts) / 3600000);
}

// Real relevance + recency, decayed together -- not two separate sorts.
// `importance` is the item's own 1-5 score when one was actually
// computed (Company News); otherwise falls back to its tier's baseline
// anchor. An item with no parseable timestamp is scored on relevance
// alone (age === null) -- never fabricate a recency bonus or penalty
// for a story whose real publish time isn't known.
function newsRelevanceScore(tier, importance, iso, now) {
  const base = importance || NEWS_BASELINE_IMPORTANCE[tier] || NEWS_BASELINE_IMPORTANCE.market;
  const age = newsAgeHours(iso, now);
  if (age === null) return base;
  return base * Math.pow(0.5, age / NEWS_RELEVANCE_HALF_LIFE_HOURS);
}

function isBreakingNews(iso, now) {
  const age = newsAgeHours(iso, now);
  return age !== null && age * 60 <= NEWS_BREAKING_MINUTES;
}

function timeAgo(iso) {
  const ts = Date.parse(iso || '');
  if (isNaN(ts)) return '';
  const diffMin = Math.round((Date.now() - ts) / 60000);
  if (diffMin < 1) return 'just now';
  if (diffMin < 60) return `${diffMin}m ago`;
  const diffH = Math.round(diffMin / 60);
  if (diffH < 24) return `${diffH}h ago`;
  return `${Math.round(diffH / 24)}d ago`;
}
